{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1527973-dark-ages-combat","id":1527973,"title":"Dark Ages Combat","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-01-04T20:52:19-08:00","id":1527973,"title":"Dark Ages Combat","content":"I've got a lot of questions about Vampire the Dark Ages 20th edition combat.<br /> <br /> 1) What attack dice pool is used to throw a punch? The main book seems to have dex plus brawl while Tome of Secrets seems to indicate it is strength plus brawl.<br /> <br /> 2) Is the +1 difficulty to attacks for having a shield a passive benefit to wearing a shield, even if you're not performing any defensive maneuvers? Without that it seems a buckler is just better.<br /> <br /> 3) If that is a passive bonus, does that mean it is difficulty 8 to attack someone wielding a sword and shield (or two swords) but only difficulty 6 to attack someone with say an axe and a dagger?<br /> <br /> 4) How do shield bashes work? Can you master craft (ala tome of secrets) a shield to make it easier to attack with?<br /> <br /> 5) While not combat oriented, this feels like a good time to ask, is there a Dark Ages 20 update of KotE? Or a V20 update at all?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":27618,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27618-shadowcatx","user":"ShadowcatX","date":". 01-05-2024, 12:07 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27618-shadowcatx","name":"ShadowcatX","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27618_2.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-05T00:16:25-08:00","id":1527991,"title":"","content":"Working through DAV20 combat rules is always fun...<br /> <br /> 1) Punching someone uses Dexterity + Brawl as standard. There are combat maneuvers (such as those in the <i>Tome of Secrets</i>) which may use Strength + Brawl, but this is not normal.<br /> <br /> 2) The +1 difficulty for anyone attacking someone with a shield applies to all attacks. It's not well defined in the rulebook, but it's clearer in earlier editions where it's described at a bit more length.<br /> <br /> 3) Attacking someone armed with a sword and shield is at +2 difficulty, so difficulty 8 as standard. I do not wholly approve of the bonus for swords, though that's the rules as written.<br /> <br /> 4) A shield is not a weapon so, rules as written, you can't mastercraft it to make it easier to attack with. As with shield bashes, probably best to make it up as you go along.<br /> <br /> 5) Best bet is to try the <i>Storyteller's Vault </i>for updates of this type.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/317-james_willoughby","name":"James_Willoughby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar317_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-05T01:09:41-08:00","id":1527998,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>James_Willoughby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1527973-dark-ages-combat?p=1527991#post1527991\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Working through DAV20 combat rules is always fun...<br /> <br /> 1) Punching someone uses Dexterity + Brawl as standard. There are combat maneuvers (such as those in the <i>Tome of Secrets</i>) which may use Strength + Brawl, but this is not normal.<br /> <br /> 2) The +1 difficulty for anyone attacking someone with a shield applies to all attacks. It's not well defined in the rulebook, but it's clearer in earlier editions where it's described at a bit more length.<br /> <br /> 3) Attacking someone armed with a sword and shield is at +2 difficulty, so difficulty 8 as standard. I do not wholly approve of the bonus for swords, though that's the rules as written.<br /> <br /> 4) A shield is not a weapon so, rules as written, you can't mastercraft it to make it easier to attack with. As with shield bashes, probably best to make it up as you go along.<br /> <br /> 5) Best bet is to try the <i>Storyteller's Vault </i>for updates of this type.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agree with everything and will add that attacks with shields are covered in the VDA20 Companion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-05T01:30:08-08:00","id":1527999,"title":"","content":"The trouble with using a shield as a weapon is it is pretty much universally worse than anything else you can do. Say you have a sword and shield; swords do a fair bit of damage, and pairing the two increases the difficulty of attacks against you, so it's a pretty good combination. But what do you do if you are disarmed and lose your sword? You want to bite your enemy, as that does Strength + 1 aggravated damage (attacking at difficulty 6 as standard), versus the Strength + 2 bashing damage of the shield (difficulty 7 as standard).<br /> <br /> Actually, the best thing you can do in most situations is to arm yourself with a sword and shield (for the additional difficulty added to the attacks of your opponents) and then bite your opponent to death.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/317-james_willoughby","name":"James_Willoughby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar317_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-05T02:56:16-08:00","id":1528003,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>James_Willoughby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1527973-dark-ages-combat?p=1527999#post1527999\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The trouble with using a shield as a weapon is it is pretty much universally worse than anything else you can do. Say you have a sword and shield; swords do a fair bit of damage, and pairing the two increases the difficulty of attacks against you, so it's a pretty good combination. But what do you do if you are disarmed and lose your sword? You want to bite your enemy, as that does Strength + 1 aggravated damage (attacking at difficulty 6 as standard), versus the Strength + 2 bashing damage of the shield (difficulty 7 as standard).<br /> <br /> Actually, the best thing you can do in most situations is to arm yourself with a sword and shield (for the additional difficulty added to the attacks of your opponents) and then bite your opponent to death.</div> </div> </div> </div>But, unless you have Typhonic Maw (Serpentis 2), you cannot bite unless you establish a clinch, hold or tackle first. And, while it is not stated explicitly as a rule, a Storyteller might object to grappling while holding two weapons (shield included).<br /> <br /> The options I have considered for my characters are:<br /> 1) use 2 swords and - using a rule for double wielding from the Companion - do 2 consecutive (no waiting for others to finish their round of actions) attacks but with the penalties for multiple actions;<br /> 2) use sword and shield for a slightly better defense against ranged attacks but lose the double wielding extra consecutive attack (as I wouldn't count the shield as a weapon for that), and you can bash the opponent with the shield if you lose the sword;<br /> 3) do a grappler-biter with Potence, but that's a significantly different build if you want it optimised;<br /> 4) if you do have Serpentis 2 or 4, you can wield 2 swords/sword and shield and do bite attacks without a grip. In fact, assuming Serpentis 4 in the hybrid form and 2 swords, you could do 2 consecutive attacks through double wielding (RAW doesn't specify that they have to be through the hand-held weapons, so they could be 2 bites or any desired combination, but I would not object to a ST limiting that by saying at least one of the attacks has to be from the handheld weapons) plus a tongue attack and a tail attack (which can be used to grapple and has extra Strength for it) later in the turn, if you can manage the increase in difficulty due to multiple actions. If you go this way, you get to combine weaponry and grappling-biting without optimising too much.<br /> <br /> Grappler-biters work best as Lamia or Warriors of Glycon with Martial Training: Brawl maneuvers because of Potence and a powered up bite. I reckon Lamia would be the most optimised. Bonsam can also pull it off with a custom bite-related or grapple-related improvement through Abombwe 2. You can enjoy heavy armours since you're going to rely on Strength more than Dexterity.<br /> <br /> More weapon oriented builds benefit more from Celerity. And Quietus Cruscitus. Poisoned attacks are always nice.<br /> <br /> Apply Martial Training maneuvers as sensible. Some are quite good.<br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-05T03:09:16-08:00","id":1528004,"title":"","content":"Ah, but clinches or holds are only resisted on the turn following the initial attack, so, if you are happy to suffer the penalty of multiple actions, you hold (with a sword and shield in your hands because why not?) then bite, and your opponent can do nothing until he or she is successfully wins a contested roll. Best way for a lot of clans to kill another Cainite with aggravated damage.<br /> <br /> I mean, a lot of this could be fixed with a few additional lines of text, but there are lots of weird outcomes in the current version of the rules.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/317-james_willoughby","name":"James_Willoughby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar317_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-05T13:57:21-08:00","id":1528057,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>James_Willoughby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1527973-dark-ages-combat?p=1528004#post1528004\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ah, but clinches or holds are only resisted on the turn following the initial attack, so, if you are happy to suffer the penalty of multiple actions, you hold (with a sword and shield in your hands because why not?) then bite, and your opponent can do nothing until he or she is successfully wins a contested roll. Best way for a lot of clans to kill another Cainite with aggravated damage.<br /> <br /> I mean, a lot of this could be fixed with a few additional lines of text, but there are lots of weird outcomes in the current version of the rules.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, but 1) I still think it's a grey area whether one can use grapple maneuvers with busy hands. I used to think it to be fine because I visualised grappling maneuver as a sort of &quot;hug&quot;, but after learning more about wrestling, I realise a grapple while holding weapons would be extremely limited and awkward, warranting at least some penalty, if allowed at all;<br /> 2) there is a limit to one attack action per turn unless one has some relevant discipline power granting more or is wielding two weapons. So grapple-bite is generally a 2 turns strategy. And I'm completely fine with it, as long as one is aware of it. In one-on-one that's not a problem, but it can be an issue when the fight involves multiple people.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-05T16:54:09-08:00","id":1528067,"title":"","content":"Pulling back to a broader point, this is why a game needs a clear, well-defined rule set, which is something that <i>Vampire: the Dark Ages</i> doesn't always have. It's reasonable to go back to the notions of common sense and the intention of the rules writers, but these will only get you so far.<br /> <br /> Let's take an uncontentious example. Raphael the Gangrel comes to a combat armed with a sword and a shield. He also activates the Feral Weapons power, and takes a claw action against his opponent, whilst relying on the sword and shield to raise the difficulty of attacks against him. The player controls Raphael makes the (correct) point that this is not explicitly ruled out within the rules of the game. I, as the Storyteller tell Raphael's player that this isn't allowed because it is clearly against both common sense and the intention of the rules. Carrying weapons does not necessarily rule out all unarmed combat maneuvers (if armed with a sword and shield you can still kick someone, after all) but you certainly shouldn't be clawing someone.<br /> <br /> But it gets a bit more murky when you get to something like a hold. All attacks are combat maneuvers, but not all combat maneuvers are attacks. A hold does no damage, so does it constitute an attack? And to take multiple actions to hold and bite another character may be difficult (depending on dice pools et cetera), but it isn't necessarily inappropriate in dramatic and narrative terms. To spring a hold on someone and then use a bite attack in a single turn is overpowered, but mostly due to the current rules for holds (71.52% success on a pool of four dice, without any further complications). <br /> <br /> It would be nice to have a tidy up of the combat rules in general, and make some of the maneuvers and other details of the system more interesting, as well as clearing out statistical anomalies caused by overage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/317-james_willoughby","name":"James_Willoughby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar317_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-06T03:06:07-08:00","id":1528094,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>James_Willoughby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1527973-dark-ages-combat?p=1528067#post1528067\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Pulling back to a broader point, this is why a game needs a clear, well-defined rule set, which is something that <i>Vampire: the Dark Ages</i> doesn't always have. It's reasonable to go back to the notions of common sense and the intention of the rules writers, but these will only get you so far.<br /> <br /> Let's take an uncontentious example. Raphael the Gangrel comes to a combat armed with a sword and a shield. He also activates the Feral Weapons power, and takes a claw action against his opponent, whilst relying on the sword and shield to raise the difficulty of attacks against him. The player controls Raphael makes the (correct) point that this is not explicitly ruled out within the rules of the game. I, as the Storyteller tell Raphael's player that this isn't allowed because it is clearly against both common sense and the intention of the rules. Carrying weapons does not necessarily rule out all unarmed combat maneuvers (if armed with a sword and shield you can still kick someone, after all) but you certainly shouldn't be clawing someone.<br /> <br /> But it gets a bit more murky when you get to something like a hold. All attacks are combat maneuvers, but not all combat maneuvers are attacks. A hold does no damage, so does it constitute an attack? And to take multiple actions to hold and bite another character may be difficult (depending on dice pools et cetera), but it isn't necessarily inappropriate in dramatic and narrative terms. To spring a hold on someone and then use a bite attack in a single turn is overpowered, but mostly due to the current rules for holds (71.52% success on a pool of four dice, without any further complications).<br /> <br /> It would be nice to have a tidy up of the combat rules in general, and make some of the maneuvers and other details of the system more interesting, as well as clearing out statistical anomalies caused by overage.</div> </div> </div> </div>I totally agree on clarifying the rules, both for combat and other things. Some say that the ST system is intentionally wobbly on mechanics to enhance narrative, but I think that's not a very good idea. First, rules enhance narrative if they allow you to better define - without disagreements and discussions at the table - the exact extent of character powers and interactions. This is itself a narrative point, not just mere dots on a sheet. Second, when players can't be sure about the rules because the ST might be applying them differently from what they understood from the book, it's hinders their plotting and imagination, compromising the game on all levels. Third, if the decisions reached to fix ambiguities in the text are not convincing for all, it can make the game less believable, and harder to bring to life in one's mind.<br /> I see a good and clear rule system as the necessary condition for a pleasant playing experience at the table that doesn't trigger discussions and disappointments.<br /> <br /> By the way, re: holds: it's true that the roll to escape comes on the next action of the victim, but one can still dodge, block or parry against the first attempt to establish the hold, clinch or tackle. That's important, balance wise. Also because an armed opponent may parry your attempt to clinch or hold or tackle by smashing his sword on your nose: if he has more successes than you, you get damaged because your attack is unarmed (I do count holds as attacks, even if they do no damage). Of course, if you have high enough Potence for automatic successes, that might not happen, but it still gives a good reason to be cautious when trying to grapple armed opponents.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}